\title{Two Structural Barriers to Classical Integer Factoring:\\ Free Witnesses and Congruence Blindness}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{13 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study five structurally distinct proposals for a classical polynomial-time integer factoring algorithm --- an evaluation (``Reed--Solomon'') code over $\Z/N$, a divisor-count-parity oracle, the reduced Burau representation of the three-strand braid group over $\Z/N$, the average-case existence of a cheaply recognizable fast subfamily of semiprimes, and a divisor congestion game --- and show that each fails, unconditionally, for one of exactly two reasons.

The first is the \emph{free-witness phenomenon}: for a semiprime $N = pq$ the Chinese Remainder isomorphism $\Z/N \cong \Z/p \times \Z/q$ turns every algebraic invariant of a structure over $\Z/N$ into a function of the two prime-level invariants. We prove that for distinct primes $p < q$ and every $k \le p$ the minimum Hamming distance of the code of evaluations of polynomials of degree at most $k$ over $\Z/N$ is exactly $N - k\max(p,q)$, so that the minimum distance of the degree-$\le 1$ code determines the complete factorization; and we prove that the image of $\sigma_1\sigma_2$ under the reduced Burau representation specialized at a unit $a$ has multiplicative order exactly $\lcmop(3,\ord(a))$, an order-finding invariant that splits as $\ord_{pq}(a) = \lcmop(\ord_p(a),\ord_q(a))$.

The second is \emph{congruence blindness}: for every modulus $m > 1$ and every bound $B$, no invariant determined by $N \bmod m$ outputs a nontrivial divisor of every semiprime $N > B$; the proof exhibits two \emph{coprime} semiprimes above $B$ in one residue class via Dirichlet's theorem. We strengthen this to bounded candidate lists. Both barriers are then applied to close the remaining proposals, including an adversary argument forcing $\Omega(m)$ queries to the divisor-parity oracle and a complete solution of the divisor congestion game, whose unique Nash equilibrium bid is the least prime factor.
\end{abstract}

