\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

Integer factoring is the paradigmatic problem whose presumed hardness underwrites deployed cryptography and whose actual complexity remains unresolved. The best classical algorithms run in subexponential but superpolynomial time; Shor's quantum algorithm runs in polynomial time; the classical picture has not moved comparably in decades.

In the absence of an unconditional lower bound, a productive posture is systematic \emph{negative} work: propose a structurally novel attack, make it mathematically precise, and determine exactly where and why it fails. Repeated across many proposals, this produces a taxonomy of obstructions.

This paper reports on five proposals, chosen from territory not previously mined for factoring attacks.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Proposal & Object & Outcome \\
\midrule
Evaluation codes & Reed--Solomon-type code over $\Z/N$ & minimum distance is a free witness \\
Divisor parity & parity of divisor counts in classes & decision-tree bound $+$ circularity \\
Braid groups & reduced Burau image of $B_3$ over $\Z/N$ & reduces exactly to order-finding \\
Average case & cheaply recognizable fast subfamily & no congruence-detectable class \\
Game theory & divisor congestion game & the equilibrium \emph{is} the factorization \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Our central finding is that all five closures factor through \textbf{two} structural facts, and only two.

\subsection{The two facts}

\textbf{(F1) CRT splitting.} For $N = pq$ with $p \ne q$ prime, $\Z/N \cong \Z/p \times \Z/q$ as rings. Any structure constructed functorially over $\Z/N$ decomposes accordingly, and every numerical invariant of it is a function of the pair of prime-level invariants. If that function can be inverted --- and in the cases we study it can --- then computing the invariant is at least as hard as separating $p$ from $q$.

\textbf{(F2) Richness of residue classes.} By Dirichlet's theorem on primes in arithmetic progressions, every class $a \bmod m$ with $\gcd(a,m)=1$ contains infinitely many primes. Hence every such class contains semiprimes with arbitrarily prescribed small factor, arbitrarily large factor gap, and pairs of semiprimes coprime to one another.

Fact (F1) makes a proposed invariant \emph{equivalent} to factoring rather than a route to it; fact (F2) kills any attempt to bypass the invariant by reading a cheap statistic off $N$.

\subsection{Terminology}

We refer informally to recurring obstruction types: \emph{circularity} (the primitive can only be evaluated by solving the problem it should solve), \emph{cost} (the quantity is factor-revealing but costs $\Omega(N)$ to obtain), \emph{known-method equivalence} (the proposal reduces to Pollard $p-1$, Fermat, or order-finding), and \emph{statistical blindness} (the $N$-only statistic is provably uncorrelated with the position of the factorization).

A \emph{free witness} is a scalar $t = t(N)$ such that $(N,t)$ determines the factorization of $N$, but such that every known evaluation of $t$ costs $\Omega(N)$ or presupposes the factorization.

Throughout, $N = pq$ denotes a semiprime with $p,q$ distinct primes, and $\minfac(N)$ the least prime factor.

\section{Preliminaries: the divisor lattice of a semiprime}

\begin{lemma}[Divisor dichotomy]\label{lem:divcases}
Let $p,q$ be primes and $d \mid pq$. Then $d \in \{1,p,q,pq\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The divisor set of a product is the product of divisor sets: $\{1,p\}\cdot\{1,q\} = \{1,q,p,pq\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Proper divisors]\label{lem:properdiv}
For primes $p,q$, the proper divisors of $pq$ are exactly $\{1,p,q\}$ (a two-element set when $p=q$).
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Lemma~\ref{lem:divcases} with $1 < p,q < pq$, valid since $p,q \ge 2$.
\end{proof}

These facts drive the parity closure, the game-theoretic closure, and the divisor-uniqueness step in the congruence-blindness arguments.

\section{Evaluation codes over $\Z/N$}

\subsection{Setup}

\begin{definition}
Let $N = pq$ with $p,q$ distinct primes. For $k \ge 1$ the \emph{evaluation code} is
\[
C_k(N) = \bigl\{ (f(x))_{x \in \Z/N} : f \in (\Z/N)[X],\ \deg f < k \bigr\} \subseteq (\Z/N)^N .
\]
For $f \in (\Z/N)[X]$ write $Z(f) = \{x : f(x) = 0\}$ and $\wt(f) = \#\{x : f(x) \ne 0\}$, so $\wt(f) + |Z(f)| = N$.
\end{definition}

Over a finite field $\F_n$ this is the classical Reed--Solomon code, of minimum distance $n - (k-1)$. Over $\Z/N$, which has zero divisors, the answer is genuinely different. Write $\pi_p : \Z/N \to \Z/p$ and $\pi_q : \Z/N \to \Z/q$ for the reductions.

\subsection{The upper bound on zeros}

\begin{lemma}[CRT injectivity]\label{lem:crtinj}
If $\gcd(p,q)=1$ then $x \mapsto (\pi_p(x), \pi_q(x))$ is injective on $\Z/pq$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
This map is the Chinese Remainder ring isomorphism $\Z/pq \to \Z/p \times \Z/q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Zero set splits]\label{lem:zsplit}
For any $f \in (\Z/N)[X]$,
\[
|Z(f)| \le |Z(\pi_p f)| \cdot |Z(\pi_q f)|,
\]
where $\pi_p f \in (\Z/p)[X]$ is the coefficientwise reduction.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $f(x) = 0$ then $(\pi_p f)(\pi_p x) = 0$ and $(\pi_q f)(\pi_q x) = 0$, so $x \mapsto (\pi_p x, \pi_q x)$ maps $Z(f)$ into $Z(\pi_p f) \times Z(\pi_q f)$; it is injective by Lemma~\ref{lem:crtinj}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Nondegeneracy]\label{lem:nondeg}
If $\pi_p f = 0$ and $\pi_q f = 0$ then $f = 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Each coefficient reduces to $0$ mod $p$ and mod $q$, hence is $0$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:crtinj}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-count bound]\label{thm:zerobound}
Let $p,q$ be distinct primes and $f \in (\Z/pq)[X]$ nonzero. Then
\[
|Z(f)| \le \deg(f)\cdot\max(p,q), \qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad \wt(f) \ge pq - \deg(f)\max(p,q).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:nondeg} at least one reduction is nonzero; say $\pi_p f \ne 0$ (the other case is symmetric). Over the field $\Z/p$ a nonzero polynomial has at most $\deg(\pi_p f) \le \deg f$ roots, so $|Z(\pi_p f)| \le \deg f$, while trivially $|Z(\pi_q f)| \le q$. Lemma~\ref{lem:zsplit} gives $|Z(f)| \le \deg(f)\, q \le \deg(f)\max(p,q)$.
\end{proof}

The bound is neither $\deg f$ (as over a field) nor $N$ (as one might fear over a ring with zero divisors), but the product $\deg f \cdot \max(p,q)$, which visibly contains a prime factor of $N$.

\subsection{Tightness}

\begin{proposition}[Extremal codeword]\label{prop:extremal}
Let $p < q$ be distinct primes and $k \le p$. Set
\[
f_k(X) = q \prod_{i=0}^{k-1} (X - i) \in (\Z/pq)[X].
\]
Then $f_k \ne 0$, $\deg f_k \le k$, and $|Z(f_k)| = kq = k\max(p,q)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The coefficient of $X^k$ is $q \bmod pq$, nonzero since $pq \nmid q$. Under the CRT identification $x \leftrightarrow (x_p,x_q)$ we have $q \leftrightarrow (q \bmod p, 0)$, so
\[
f_k(x) \longleftrightarrow \Bigl( (q\bmod p)\textstyle\prod_{i<k}(x_p - i),\ 0\Bigr).
\]
The second coordinate always vanishes; the first vanishes whenever $x_p \in \{0,\dots,k-1\}$, which are $k$ distinct elements of $\Z/p$ because $k \le p$. Each such class contains exactly $q$ elements of $\Z/pq$, so $|Z(f_k)| \ge kq$. The reverse inequality is Theorem~\ref{thm:zerobound}.
\end{proof}

The extremal codeword vanishes on \emph{complete residue classes modulo $p$}, each with exactly $q$ lifts: the zero-set spacing is what reveals the factorization, and the count merely records it.

\begin{theorem}[Exact minimum distance]\label{thm:mindist}
Let $p < q$ be distinct primes, $N = pq$, and $1 \le k \le p$. Then every nonzero $f$ of degree $\le k$ satisfies $\wt(f) \ge N - k\max(p,q)$, and some nonzero $f$ of degree $\le k$ attains equality. Hence the minimum distance of the code of evaluations of polynomials of degree $\le k$ is exactly
\[
d = N - k\max(p,q) = N - kq .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:zerobound} with $\deg f \le k$, and Proposition~\ref{prop:extremal} with $\wt = N - |Z|$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The free witness}

\begin{theorem}[Minimum distance reveals the factorization]\label{thm:freewitness-rs}
Let $p < q$ with $N = pq$ and let $t \in \N$ satisfy: $|Z(f)| \le t$ for every nonzero $f$ of degree $\le 1$, and $|Z(g)| \ge t$ for some nonzero $g$ of degree $\le 1$. Then $t = q$ and $N/t = p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:zerobound} gives $t \le |Z(g)| \le \max(p,q) = q$. Proposition~\ref{prop:extremal} with $k=1$ produces $f_1$ with $|Z(f_1)| = q$, whence $q \le t$. So $t = q$ and $N/t = p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The minimum distance $d$ of the degree-$\le 1$ evaluation code satisfies $N - d = \max(p,q)$ and $N/(N-d) = \min(p,q)$. An algorithm computing $d$ in time $T$ yields a factoring algorithm in time $T + O(\operatorname{polylog} N)$.
\end{corollary}

The two available routes to $d$ are the formula (presupposing the factorization) and exhaustive weight search over a codeword space of size $\ge N^k$. Both are barriers; no third route is known.

\begin{remark}
The same phenomenon has been observed for norm counts in quadratic orders, group-order counts, class counts of binary quadratic forms, counts of subgroups of Heisenberg-type groups, and modular indices attached to cusps. The coding-theoretic setting is structurally distinct --- the invariant is metric rather than a cardinality of an algebraic set --- yet the mechanism is identical.
\end{remark}

\section{The divisor-count-parity primitive}

\begin{definition}
For $N, m, a$ with $m \ge 1$, set
\[
P(N,m,a) = \#\{d : d \text{ a proper divisor of } N,\ d \equiv a \bmod m\} \bmod 2 \in \{0,1\}.
\]
A semiprime $N = pq$ is \emph{non-degenerate at $m$} if $1, p, q$ are pairwise incongruent mod $m$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Support characterization]\label{thm:support}
Let $N = pq$ be non-degenerate at $m$. Then $P(N,m,a) = 1$ if and only if $a \equiv 1$, $p$ or $q \pmod m$, and
\[
\{a \in \{0,\dots,m-1\} : P(N,m,a) = 1\} = \{1 \bmod m,\ p \bmod m,\ q \bmod m\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:properdiv} the proper divisors are exactly $\{1,p,q\}$, lying by non-degeneracy in three distinct classes. The filtered set therefore has cardinality $1$ at a marked class and $0$ elsewhere.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Factor-residue recovery]\label{cor:recover}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:support}, deleting the a priori known class $1 \bmod m$ from the support yields exactly $\{p \bmod m,\ q \bmod m\}$. The support has exactly three elements, so the density of informative classes is $3/m$.
\end{corollary}

The primitive is thus a genuine factorization certificate modulo $m$; querying enough moduli and reassembling by CRT determines $p$ and $q$. The interesting question is its cost.

\begin{theorem}[Adversary indistinguishability]\label{thm:adversary}
Let $N = pq$ and $N' = p'q'$ be semiprimes, both non-degenerate at $m$, and let $Q$ be a query set with
\[
a \bmod m \notin \{1,p,q\}\bmod m \quad\text{and}\quad a \bmod m \notin \{1,p',q'\}\bmod m \qquad (a \in Q).
\]
Then $P(N,m,a) = P(N',m,a)$ for all $a \in Q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:support} both sides vanish at every $a \in Q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Query lower bound]
The union of the marked classes of two semiprimes has at most six elements out of $m$. An adversary answering $0$ until forced compels $m-6$ queries, so $\Omega(m)$ queries are necessary in the worst case; with $m$ comparable to $q \approx \sqrt N$ this is an $\Omega(\sqrt N)$ bound. Independently, evaluating a single query requires the divisors of $N$, i.e.\ factoring.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Collision case]\label{thm:collision}
Let $p \ne q$ be primes with $p \equiv q \pmod m$ and $1 \not\equiv p \pmod m$. Then
\[
\{a \in \{0,\dots,m-1\} : P(pq,m,a) = 1\} = \{1 \bmod m\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At $a \equiv p$ the filtered set is $\{p,q\}$, of even size, so the parity vanishes; at $a \equiv 1$ it is $\{1\}$; elsewhere it is empty.
\end{proof}

The failures are exactly the merged-class cases: structural, not accidental, and genuinely unresolvable. For $N = 15$, $m = 7$ the support is $\{1,3,5\}$ and the factor residues $\{3,5\}$ are recovered; for $m = 2$, where $3 \equiv 5 \equiv 1$, the support degenerates to $\{1\}$.

\section{Braid groups: the Burau image is order-finding}

\begin{definition}
Over a commutative ring $R$ and for $a \in R$ set
\[
r(\sigma_1) = \begin{pmatrix} -a & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}, \qquad
r(\sigma_2) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ a & -a\end{pmatrix}, \qquad
B_a := r(\sigma_1) r(\sigma_2) = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -a \\ a & -a\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Braid relation]
$r(\sigma_1) r(\sigma_2) r(\sigma_1) = r(\sigma_2) r(\sigma_1) r(\sigma_2)$ over any commutative ring.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Direct $2\times 2$ matrix computation.
\end{proof}

This is an honest non-abelian two-generator picture, of the kind one hopes might evade the commutative barriers. It does not.

\begin{theorem}[Full-twist scalar]\label{thm:fulltwist}
$B_a^3 = a^3 \cdot I$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Direct computation. Conceptually, $(\sigma_1\sigma_2)^3 = \Delta^2$ is the full twist generating the centre of $B_3$, and central elements act by scalars.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cubepow}
For all $s \ge 0$, $B_a^{3s} = a^{3s} I$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $s$ using Theorem~\ref{thm:fulltwist} and centrality of scalars.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Order equation]\label{thm:order}
Let $R$ be a nontrivial commutative ring and $a \in R$ a unit. Then for all $n \ge 0$,
\[
B_a^{\,n} = I \iff \bigl(3 \mid n \ \text{and}\ a^n = 1\bigr),
\qquad\text{hence}\qquad
\ord(B_a) = \lcmop\bigl(3, \ord(a)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) If $n = 3s$ and $a^n = 1$ then $B_a^n = a^n I = I$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:cubepow}.

($\Rightarrow$) Write $n = 3s+t$ with $t \in \{0,1,2\}$; then $B_a^n = a^{3s} B_a^t$. Comparing $(0,1)$ entries with $I$ gives $a^{3s} (B_a^t)_{01} = 0$, and $a^{3s}$ is a unit, so $(B_a^t)_{01} = 0$. But $(B_a)_{01} = -a \ne 0$ and $(B_a^2)_{01} = a^2 \ne 0$ since $a$ is a unit in a nontrivial ring. Hence $t = 0$, so $3 \mid n$, and then $a^n I = I$ gives $a^n = 1$ on comparing $(0,0)$ entries.

For the order formula, $\lcmop(3,\ord(a))$ satisfies both conditions, so $\ord(B_a)$ divides it; and applying the equivalence to $n = \ord(B_a)$ gives $3 \mid n$ and $\ord(a) \mid n$, so the lcm divides $\ord(B_a)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Two-way reduction]
For a unit $a$: $\ord(a) \mid \ord(B_a)$ and $\ord(B_a) \mid 3\ord(a)$. Computing braid orders and computing multiplicative orders are the same problem up to a factor of $3$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[CRT splitting of order]\label{thm:ordcrt}
Let $p,q$ be coprime and $a \in \Z/pq$. Then
\[
\ord_{pq}(a) = \lcmop\bigl(\ord_p(\pi_p a),\ \ord_q(\pi_q a)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The CRT map is a ring isomorphism, hence an isomorphism of multiplicative monoids, hence order-preserving; in a direct product the order of a pair is the lcm of the component orders.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For a unit $a$ modulo $N = pq$, $\ord(B_a) = \lcmop(3, \ord_p(a), \ord_q(a))$.
\end{corollary}

This exhibits the braid-order invariant as an aggregate of the two prime-level orders --- the datum exploited by Pollard's $p-1$ method when smooth, and computed by Shor's algorithm. It is not a new capability but the known dead end.

\begin{theorem}[Lagrange for the Burau image]\label{thm:lagrange}
Let $a$ be a unit modulo $N > 1$, and let $H_a = \langle r(\sigma_1), r(\sigma_2)\rangle \le \GL_2(\Z/N)$ (both generators are invertible, of determinant $-a$). Then
\[
\lcmop(3, \ord_N(a)) \ \big|\ |H_a|, \qquad\text{in particular}\qquad \ord_N(a) \ \big|\ |H_a| .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The element $B_a = r(\sigma_1)r(\sigma_2)$ lies in $H_a$ and has order $\lcmop(3,\ord(a))$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:order}; apply Lagrange's theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Experimentally $|H_a|$ carries \emph{more} information than $\ord_N(a)$: modulo $N = 21$, both $a = 2$ and $a = 5$ give $\lcmop(\ord_3(a),\ord_7(a)) = 6$, yet the two images have different cardinalities ($336$ versus $24$). The group order is sensitive to the individual pair $(\ord_p(a),\ord_q(a))$, not merely their lcm. That refinement points in exactly the unavailable direction: knowing the pair separately is strictly closer to knowing $p$ and $q$, so the extra sensitivity makes the invariant harder to compute, not easier. The one genuinely non-commutative feature --- that the swap $p \leftrightarrow q$ is not realized by any braid, so the representation does not symmetrize the prime-level data --- is what permits the separation, but separation is not extraction.
\end{remark}

\section{Congruence blindness}

\begin{theorem}[Class population]\label{thm:classpop}
Let $m \ge 1$, let $a \in \Z/m$ be a unit, let $p$ be a prime invertible mod $m$, and let $B \in \N$. Then there is a prime $r > \max(B,p)$ with
\[
pr \equiv a \pmod m \qquad\text{and}\qquad \minfac(pr) = p .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$p^{-1}a$ is a unit mod $m$; by Dirichlet's theorem choose a prime $r \equiv p^{-1}a \pmod m$ with $r > \max(B,p)$. Then $pr \equiv a$, and since $p < r$ are prime, $\minfac(pr) = p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Unboundedness inside a class]\label{cor:unbounded}
Fix $m$, a unit class $a$ and a bound $B$. Within the class $a \bmod m$ there are semiprimes $N = pr$ with gap $r - p > B$, and semiprimes with least prime factor $p$ for every prime $p$ invertible mod $m$.
\end{corollary}

This closes the most plausible average-case loophole: the Fermat-easy family (small $|p-q|$) is not congruence-detectable, since every unit class contains semiprimes with arbitrarily large gaps.

\begin{definition}
An invariant $I : \N \to \N$ is \emph{congruence-determined modulo $m$} if $N \equiv N' \pmod m$ implies $I(N) = I(N')$. It is \emph{factor-revealing beyond $B$} if for all primes $p < r$ with $pr > B$ we have $I(pr) \mid pr$ and $1 < I(pr) < pr$.
\end{definition}

Any statistic computed from finitely many congruence conditions on $N$ --- parities, $N \bmod 8$, Jacobi symbols with fixed numerator, last-digit patterns --- is congruence-determined modulo the lcm of the moduli involved.

\begin{proposition}[Non-vacuity]
The map $N \mapsto \minfac(N)$ is factor-revealing beyond $0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For primes $p < r$, $\minfac(pr) = p$, which divides $pr$ with $1 < p < pr$.
\end{proof}

Thus the meta-theorem below is an obstruction on the congruence side, not a triviality arising from an unsatisfiable factor-revealing condition.

\begin{theorem}[Coprime semiprimes in one class]\label{thm:coprimepair}
For every $m > 1$ and every $B$ there exist primes $p_1 < r_1$ and $p_2 < r_2$, all four distinct, with $p_1 r_1 > B$, $p_2 r_2 > B$ and $p_1 r_1 \equiv p_2 r_2 \pmod m$. In particular $\gcd(p_1r_1, p_2r_2) = 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Dirichlet choose primes $p_1 < p_2$ with $p_1 \equiv p_2 \equiv 1 \pmod m$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:classpop} with $a = 1$ to $p_1$ (threshold $\max(B,p_2)$) to get $r_1$, then to $p_2$ (threshold $\max(B,r_1)$) to get $r_2$. This forces $p_1 < p_2 < r_1 < r_2$, so the four primes are distinct, and both products lie in the class $1 \bmod m$ above $B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Free-witness meta-theorem]\label{thm:meta}
Let $m > 1$ and $B \in \N$. No invariant congruence-determined modulo $m$ is factor-revealing beyond $B$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $I$ were both, and take $N_1 = p_1r_1$, $N_2 = p_2r_2$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:coprimepair}. Since $N_1 \equiv N_2$, we have $d := I(N_1) = I(N_2)$. Factor-revealing plus Lemma~\ref{lem:divcases} gives $d \in \{p_1,r_1\}$ and $d \in \{p_2,r_2\}$; but the four primes are distinct.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:nodetector}
For every $m > 1$ and $B$: there is no $D : \Z/m \to \N$ with $D(N \bmod m) = \minfac(N)$ for all semiprimes $N > B$; nor with $D(N \bmod m) = \max(p,q)$ for all semiprimes $N = pq > B$. In particular the quantity $N - d(C_2(N)) = \max(p,q)$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:freewitness-rs} and the equilibrium bid of the divisor congestion game are both non-congruence-determined.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Each of $\max(p,q)$ and $\minfac(N)$ is a nontrivial divisor, so such a $D$ would be factor-revealing; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:meta}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Interpretation: the average-case question}

The average-case proposal asks whether there is a density-one family of semiprimes, recognizable from $N$ alone in polynomial time, factoring below the $\sqrt N$ floor by a non-smoothness mechanism. Corollary~\ref{cor:unbounded} and Corollary~\ref{cor:nodetector} close the congruence-recognizable case unconditionally. This matches the experimental record: across large samples, Pollard-$\rho$ step counts are statistically indistinguishable across the classes $N \bmod 4$, $N \bmod 8$ and the Jacobi symbol $(2/N)$, whereas the genuinely fast subfamily (small $|p-q|$) shows a dramatic effect and is provably invisible from $N$. The remaining known fast families --- smooth $p-1$, small gap --- are measure-zero and are known methods in disguise.

\section{Bounded candidate lists}

A natural relaxation lets the congruence-determined function output a short \emph{list} of candidates, one of which works; a list of length $L$ would give a factoring algorithm at the price of $L$ trial divisions.

\begin{theorem}[Pairwise-coprime family in one class]\label{thm:family}
For every $m > 1$, every $k$ and every $B$ there are primes
\[
p_0 < r_0 < p_1 < r_1 < \cdots < p_{k-1} < r_{k-1},
\]
all exceeding $B$ and all congruent to $1 \bmod m$. The $k$ semiprimes $N_i = p_i r_i$ are then pairwise coprime, all $> B$, and all $\equiv 1 \pmod m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$, drawing each prime by Dirichlet's theorem from the class $1 \bmod m$ above the previous one. Strict increase makes the $2k$ primes distinct, hence the products pairwise coprime.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No bounded congruence-determined candidate list]\label{thm:lists}
Fix $m > 1$, $k \in \N$ and $B \in \N$, and let $S : \Z/m \to \{\text{finite subsets of }\N\}$ satisfy $|S(a)| \le k$ for all $a$. Then some semiprime $N > B$ has no nontrivial divisor inside $S(N \bmod m)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose otherwise. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:family} with parameters $k+1$ and $B$, obtaining pairwise-coprime semiprimes $N_0,\dots,N_k > B$ all in the class $1 \bmod m$. For each $i$ the hypothesis yields $f(i) \in S(1)$ with $f(i) \mid N_i$ and $1 < f(i) < N_i$, hence $f(i) \in \{p_i, r_i\}$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:divcases}. Since the blocks are strictly increasing, $i < j$ implies $f(i) \le r_i < p_j \le f(j)$, so $f$ is injective and $|S(1)| \ge k+1$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Thus a congruence-determined candidate list containing a factor of every large semiprime must have unbounded length, and is therefore not an algorithm.

\section{The divisor congestion game}

\begin{definition}
For $N \ge 1$ the \emph{divisor congestion game} has admissible bids $d \in \{2,\dots,N-1\}$ and payoff
\[
w_N(d) = \begin{cases} N/d, & d \mid N,\\ -N, & d \nmid N.\end{cases}
\]
A bid $d$ is a \emph{best response} if $w_N(e) \le w_N(d)$ for every admissible $e$.
\end{definition}

The game is symmetric with no interaction term, so a profile is a Nash equilibrium exactly when every player plays a best response.

\begin{theorem}[A payoff query is a divisibility test]\label{thm:payoffdvd}
For $N > 0$: $w_N(d) \ge 0 \iff d \mid N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $d \mid N$ then $w_N(d) = N/d \ge 0$; otherwise $w_N(d) = -N < 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Flat landscape]\label{thm:flat}
If $d \nmid N$ and $e \nmid N$ then $w_N(d) = w_N(e) = -N$.
\end{theorem}

The payoff surface is exactly constant off the divisor set: no gradient exists, and no local-search or hill-climbing procedure can be guided toward a divisor. The landscape is a plateau of size $\approx N$ punctuated by isolated pits.

\begin{theorem}[Best response]\label{thm:bestresp}
Let $N$ be composite with $\minfac(N) < N$. Then $w_N(d) \le w_N(\minfac(N))$ for every admissible bid $d$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $d \nmid N$ then $w_N(d) = -N \le 0 \le N/\minfac(N)$. If $d \mid N$ with $d \ge 2$ then $\minfac(N) \le d$, so $N/d \le N/\minfac(N)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness]\label{thm:unique}
Let $N$ be composite with $\minfac(N) < N$ and let $d$ be an admissible best response. Then $d = \minfac(N)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Best response gives $w_N(\minfac(N)) \le w_N(d)$; the left side is $N/\minfac(N) \ge 0$, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:payoffdvd} $d \mid N$, whence $N/\minfac(N) \le N/d$. Combined with $\minfac(N) \le d$, which gives the reverse inequality, the cofactors are equal; multiplying each divisor by its (positive) cofactor to get $N$ and cancelling gives $d = \minfac(N)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The equilibrium is the factorization]\label{thm:equilfact}
Let $N = pq$ with $p < q$ prime and let $d$ be an admissible best response. Then $d = p$, $w_N(d) = q$, and
\[
N = d \cdot w_N(d),
\]
with $d$ prime and $1 < d < N$. Reading off the equilibrium yields the complete factorization.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Specialize Theorem~\ref{thm:unique} to $N = pq$, where $\minfac(N) = p$ and $w_N(p) = q$.
\end{proof}

Hence the game admits a unique equilibrium, computable in polynomial time \emph{given} the factorization, encoding the factorization exactly. It is a restatement, not an algorithm: evaluating any payoff is a divisibility test (Theorem~\ref{thm:payoffdvd}), so a best-response scan is trial division at cost $\Theta(N)$; the landscape is exactly flat off the divisors (Theorem~\ref{thm:flat}), so better-response dynamics have no signal; and the equilibrium bid $\minfac(N)$ is not congruence-determined (Corollary~\ref{cor:nodetector}), so no residue shortcut applies. Verification is polynomial time; discovery is the original problem. For $N = 91 = 7\cdot13$ the bid $7$ has payoff $13$ and dominates every other admissible bid.

\section{Algorithms}

We record the algorithmic content as procedures. All are reductions or verifications; none is a factoring speedup, which is the point.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Factor from minimum distance.} Given $N$ and the minimum distance $d$ of the degree-$\le 1$ evaluation code, output $q \leftarrow N-d$ and $p \leftarrow N/q$. Correct by Theorem~\ref{thm:freewitness-rs}; cost $O(\operatorname{polylog} N)$ given $d$, while obtaining $d$ costs $\Omega(N)$ by exhaustive weight search.
\item \textbf{Factor-residue recovery.} Given oracle access to $P(N,m,\cdot)$, query all $a < m$, collect the support $S$, return $S \setminus \{1 \bmod m\}$. Correct by Corollary~\ref{cor:recover}; $\Omega(m)$ queries are necessary by Theorem~\ref{thm:adversary}, and each query is itself a factorization.
\item \textbf{Braid order.} Return $\lcmop(3,\ord_N(a))$; conversely, from the braid order $L$ recover $\ord_N(a)$ as $L$ or $L/3$ by testing $a^{L/3} = 1$. Cost identical to order-finding up to a constant.
\item \textbf{Congruence-blindness witness.} Given $m > 1$ and $B$, search primes $\equiv 1 \bmod m$ in increasing order to build $p_1 < p_2 < r_1 < r_2$ as in Theorem~\ref{thm:coprimepair}; the pair $(p_1r_1, p_2r_2)$ defeats any congruence-determined divisor predictor, and extending to $k+1$ blocks defeats any list of length $\le k$.
\item \textbf{Game equilibrium.} Scan $d \in \{2,\dots,N-1\}$ maximizing $w_N(d)$. Correct by Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}; cost $\Theta(N)$ divisibility tests, i.e.\ trial division.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Six settings, one phenomenon}

The free-witness phenomenon has now been exhibited in six structurally distinct settings: norm counts in quadratic orders; group-order counts; class counts of binary quadratic forms; counts of subgroups in Heisenberg-type groups; modular indices attached to cusps; and, new here, minimum distances of evaluation codes. The invariants are of different mathematical types and the constructions are unrelated, yet in each case a scalar $t$ arises such that $(N,t)$ determines the factorization and is reachable only at cost $\Omega(N)$ or via the factorization itself.

The unifying explanation is CRT splitting, and its scope is broad: any invariant defined functorially from $\Z/N$ inherits the product decomposition and is a function of the prime-level pair. To be useful it must be computable \emph{without} the splitting, and sensitivity to the splitting and computability appear to be in direct tension.

\subsection{What the two facts do not prove}

Neither fact implies that factoring is hard, and we make no such claim. Theorem~\ref{thm:meta} rules out congruence-determined predictors, not arbitrary polynomial-time predictors: a polynomial-time algorithm may compute a function of $N$ not determined by any fixed residue class. Similarly the free-witness results show that particular invariants are equivalent to factoring, not that no invariant is easier. The framework is diagnostic: it identifies, for a new proposal, which of two well-understood walls it is about to hit.

\subsection{Sharpness}

Theorem~\ref{thm:mindist} is an equality valid for all $k \le p$, with an explicit extremal codeword. Theorem~\ref{thm:order} is an exact order formula, the factor $3$ reflecting the centre of $B_3$. Theorem~\ref{thm:collision} shows the failures of the parity primitive are exactly the merged-class cases, so nothing is lost by the non-degeneracy hypothesis. Theorem~\ref{thm:lists} is tight in $k$: the witnessing family has exactly $k+1$ members.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[CRT-splitting dichotomy for evaluation codes]
For every $k \le \min(p,q)$ and every $N = pq$, the full weight enumerator of $C_k(N)$ --- not merely its minimum distance --- is the Hadamard product of the two prime-level Reed--Solomon weight enumerators, and every gap in the spectrum (a weight not attained) determines $\max(p,q)$. Consequently a polynomial-time algorithm for any single nonzero coefficient of the weight enumerator would yield a factoring algorithm.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that Theorem~\ref{thm:zerobound} is really a statement about the product structure of the zero set (Lemma~\ref{lem:zsplit}): the whole weight spectrum is a product of prime-level spectra, so every spectral feature is a free witness. The minimum-distance case is fully settled (Theorem~\ref{thm:mindist}, exact for all $k \le p$); the general spectrum requires only a counting refinement of the same CRT injection.

\begin{conjecture}[Beyond congruences]
Theorem~\ref{thm:meta} generalizes from ``determined by $N \bmod m$'' to ``determined by the value of a fixed polynomial-size arithmetic circuit on the digits of $N$'': no such invariant names a nontrivial factor of every large semiprime, unless factoring is in polynomial time.
\end{conjecture}

The Dirichlet argument used only one property of the class $\{N : N \equiv a \bmod m\}$ --- that it contains two coprime semiprimes. Any invariant whose level sets contain two coprime semiprimes is blind in the same way, and level sets of small circuits are large. The meta-theorem is already stated for an arbitrary invariant; only the ``level sets are rich'' input must be replaced, turning a number-theoretic lemma into a combinatorial one. The list version is now settled (Theorem~\ref{thm:lists}); what remains open is replacing ``congruence-determined'' by ``circuit-determined''.

\begin{conjecture}[Braid invariants beyond the centre]
Every isomorphism invariant of $H_a$ is determined by the pair $(\ord_p(a),\ord_q(a))$ together with $p$ and $q$, and computing any of them is order-finding-hard.
\end{conjecture}

Theorem~\ref{thm:order} concerns the specific element $\sigma_1\sigma_2$, whose cube is central; the question is whether conjugacy class sizes, the derived series, or the trace spectrum of $H_a$ carry information beyond the prime-level order pair, and whether any of it is computable without order-finding.

Two further directions. First, the randomized and quantum query complexities of the divisor-parity primitive are open, as are the analogues for $P_j(N,m,a) = \#\{d \mid N : d \equiv a\} \bmod j$ with $j > 2$ and for weighted variants such as $\sum_{d \equiv a} d$. Second, the divisor congestion game is closed because its landscape is exactly flat off the divisors; an interpolating payoff such as $w(d) = -\min_{e \mid N}|d - e|$ or $w(d) = -(N \bmod d)$ would create genuine gradients, and the question is whether any such interpolation is both computable in polynomial time from $N$ alone and endowed with polynomially converging better-response dynamics. The two requirements appear to conflict, and formulating that conflict as a theorem is the natural next step.

\section{Conclusion}

Five proposals, drawn from coding theory, divisor combinatorics, braid groups, average-case analysis, and game theory, have been analyzed to completion. Each fails, and each fails for one of two reasons: either the Chinese Remainder splitting converts the proposed invariant into a free witness --- a quantity that \emph{is} the factorization, reachable only at cost $\Omega(N)$ --- or Dirichlet's theorem renders the proposed cheap statistic blind to the factorization entirely.

The results are unconditional and exact where exactness is available: the minimum distance of the evaluation code over $\Z/pq$ is precisely $N - k\max(p,q)$; the braid element $\sigma_1\sigma_2$ has order precisely $\lcmop(3,\ord(a))$; the divisor-parity support is precisely three residue classes; the game's unique equilibrium bid is precisely the least prime factor; and no congruence-determined function, or bounded-length list of functions, names a nontrivial divisor of every large semiprime.

What emerges is not a hardness proof but a map. Any future proposal that constructs an algebraic invariant over $\Z/N$ should expect the splitting to convert it into a free witness; any proposal that reads a statistic off $N$ should expect Dirichlet to blind it. An idea that escapes must escape both --- and articulating how is now a precise and answerable demand.

